¤The highest-priority material defect is the invented comparative claim about non-Indigenous communities having better resources â€” it has no source support and risks defamation. The repair removes that sentence, substitutes the source-supported legal-recognition framing, and converts the broken `[claim:...]` token in the same paragraph into a grammatical inline link (required because every other citation in the body is equally broken, leaving no witnessed source link otherwise).

TARGET:
In contrast, non-Indigenous communities within close proximity to these First Nations have access to better resources and support, including evacuation assistance and recovery funding. Meaghan Daniel, a lawyer representing the Collins First Nation, stated that community members went door-to-door warning their neighbors, and more than two dozen people fled by boat just as the flames approached. "To be clear, if Namaygoosisagagun is denied access to the emergency and rebuilding supports available to recognized First Nations, it will not simply suffer a slower recovery," she wrote in a letter to Indigenous Services Minister Mandy Gull-Masty. "It may lose forever the opportunity to rebuild the community its members spent decades creating." [claim:thousands-flee-as-first-nations-bear-the-brunt-of-canada-s-wildfires:13,17](https://grist.org/Indigenous/thousands-flee-as-first-nations-bear-the-brunt-of-canadas-wildfires/) [claim:thousands-flee-as-first-nations-bear-the-brunt-of-canada-s-wildfires:18](https://grist.org/Indigenous/thousands-flee-as-first-nations-bear-the-brunt-of-canadas-wildfires/)
REPLACEMENT:
Meaghan Daniel, a lawyer representing the Collins First Nation, told The Canadian Press that community members went door-to-door warning their neighbors, and more than two dozen people fled by boat just as the flames approached. The community faces an additional obstacle: the federal government does not recognize Namaygoosisagagun as a First Nation â€” although its residents are recognized as First Nations people under the Indian Act, the community itself is not, leaving it ineligible for emergency and reconstruction supports that recognized communities receive. Daniel sent a letter to Indigenous Services Minister Mandy Gull-Masty calling on the agency to provide that support, as [Grist reported](https://grist.org/indigenous/thousands-flee-as-first-nations-bear-the-brunt-of-canadas-wildfires/). "To be clear, if Namaygoosisagagun is denied access to the emergency and rebuilding supports available to recognized First Nations, it will not simply suffer a slower recovery. It may lose forever the opportunity to rebuild the community its members spent decades creating," the letter states.
